 "note": "Land to government. The key is the Census's own AIANNH code, which a reservation and its off-reservation trust land share. Governments are named exactly as the Bureau of Indian Affairs names them in data/bia-tribal-governments.json. Kentucky is covered with NO AREAS, which is a measurement rather than an omission: all six AIANNH layers return zero features over the state's whole extent, with North Carolina run as a control on the same six queries and answering 7, 2 and 2 on three of them, so the queries work. Re-measured 2026-09-30 at that instance's go-live. OKLAHOMA IS THE ONE STATE WHERE STATISTICAL AREAS ARE ENTERED (Adam, 2026-10-10, \"Make an exception for OK\"): its Oklahoma Tribal Statistical Areas and joint-use areas carry land classes `otsa` and `joint-use`, and tribal_areas.DRAWN_STATISTICAL is what allows them. Where the Census's own name for one area names several nations, the entry lists each under `governments`, matched to the Bureau's list by that name; nations seated inside an area whose name does not name them (the United Keetoowah Band, the Delaware Tribe of Indians and the Alabama-Quassarte, Kialegee and Thlopthlocco tribal towns) are not entered here; scripts/build_tribal_areas.py places them by their seat city and the card lists them as seated in that area. The Shawnee Trust Land is matched to the Shawnee Tribe by the Census's basename.",
